PROMISES

Promise = work still running. Host runtime's own promises, so JavaScript
semantics: settles once, keeps result, awaiting twice gives same answer,
familiar combinators.
- (await promise [timeout-ms]) wait, return value, re-raise error. Settled
  promise returns at once.
- (promise-all promises) one promise for every value, in order; rejects when
  any does.
- (promise-all-settled promises) one promise for each outcome,
(:fulfilled value) or (:rejected "message") ; never rejects, so one failure
  keeps siblings. Use when loading several servers.
- (promise-any promises) first to succeed · (promise-race promises)
first to settle either way.
- (promise-state promise) :pending, :fulfilled or :rejected, no wait.
- (promises) list what still runs · (cancel promise) abort work behind one.

GOTCHAS

- Hash-and-angle-brackets printout of promise cannot be read back: typing one
  is always an error. Awaiting it is the common case; (await load-mcp-1) is
  what it meant.

RULES

- A PROMISE IS A LIVE VALUE, AND AN UNAWAITED LOAD IS DONE. Promise-returning call installs its result itself on settle. Step reporting load-mcp-1: a promise, still running … is COMPLETE: carry on, use what it loaded later. Await only to use value in same step; wrap the single call: (await (load-mcp "acme")) . Never write a #<…> form — #<promise>, #<secret:KEY> — back into code: (await #<promise>) is an error. Refer to promise by name REPL reported ((await load-mcp-1)) or one you bound.
